Two Pointers
  Two Pointers is a technique where two variables (pointers/indexes) are used to traverse a data structure (mostly arrays or strings).
  Instead of using nested loops, we move pointers smartly to reduce time complexity.

Use of two pointers
  Reduces unnecessary comparisons
  Optimizes brute force solutions
  Mostly converts O(n²) → O(n)
  Very common in coding interviews and LeetCode

Main Idea
  We use:
    Left Pointer
    Right Pointer
  and move them based on conditions.

Types of Two Pointer 
  There are 3 types of two pinters in it:
  Approach	            Description
  Opposite Direction	  One pointer at start, one at end
  Same Direction	      Both move forward
  Fast & Slow Pointer  	One moves faster than other

1.Opposite Direction
  Used mostly in:
    Sorted arrays
    Pair sum problems
    Palindrome checking
Example
  Find if two numbers sum to target.
  arr = [1,2,3,4,6]
  target = 6
Approach
  left = start
  right = end
conditions:
  sum < target → move left
  sum > target → move right
  sum == target → found
Time Complexity
  Operation	  Complexity
  Traversal    	O(n)
  Space        	O(1)

2. Same Direction Two Pointers
Both pointers move forward.
Used in:
  Removing duplicates
  Partitioning arrays
  Moving zeros
Example: 
  Remove Duplicates
  arr = [1,1,2,2,3]
   if arr[i] != arr[j]:
    i += 1
    arr[i] = arr[j]
Working
i stores unique elements
j scans array
Time Complexity
Complexity  	Value
Time	        O(n)
Space	        O(1)

3. Fast and Slow Pointer
One pointer moves faster than the other.
Used in:
  Linked Lists
  Cycle Detection
  Middle Element Finding
Time Complexity
Complexity	Value
Time         O(n)
Space	       O(1)


Advantages
  Very efficient
  Reduces nested loops
  Saves memory
  Easy once patterns understood
Disadvantages
  Sometimes difficult to identify
  Mostly useful only in linear structures
